<Base title="Guide: Choosing a Bookkeeper" description="The questions to ask any bookkeeper or bookkeeping firm before you hire them: who does the work, what the monthly process includes, who owns your books, how pricing works, and the warning signs to watch for.">
  <div class="container">
    <section class="hero hero-full hero-center">
      <span class="eyebrow boxed">Guide · Hiring</span>
      <h1>Choosing a <span class="gold-text">Bookkeeper.</span></h1>
      <p class="lead">The questions to ask any bookkeeper or firm before you hire them, what good answers sound like, and the warning signs worth paying attention to.</p>
    </section>

    <article class="article">
      <aside class="glance">
        <h2>At a Glance</h2>
        <dl>
          <div><dt>Who it's for</dt><dd>Owners hiring a bookkeeper for the first time or replacing one</dd></div>
          <div><dt>What to compare</dt><dd>Who does the work, the monthly process, communication, ownership of your books, and pricing</dd></div>
          <div><dt>The key question</dt><dd>What exactly happens every month, and by when</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Often overlooked</dt><dd>Who owns your accounting file and what happens if you leave</dd></div>
          <div><dt>How to use it</dt><dd>Bring the question list at the end to every conversation</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </aside>

      <h2>Why the Choice Matters</h2>
      <p>Bookkeeping can look like a commodity. Every provider says they will categorize transactions and send reports, and prices vary widely for what sounds like the same service. The differences show up later: in whether the books are actually reconciled, whether reports arrive on time, whether anyone catches a mistake before your tax preparer does, and how easy it is to get a straight answer.</p>
      <p>Switching bookkeepers is possible, but it costs time. Records have to be handed over, the new provider has to learn the business, and sometimes past work has to be redone. It is worth asking good questions up front.</p>

      <h2>The Main Types of Bookkeepers</h2>
      <p>There is no single right choice. Each option suits different businesses.</p>
      <h3>Part-time or in-house employee</h3>
      <p>Someone on your payroll who handles bookkeeping, often alongside other office work. You get someone who knows the business well and is close at hand. The risks are that bookkeeping gets pushed aside by other duties, no one reviews their work, and everything depends on one person staying.</p>
      <h3>Independent freelancer</h3>
      <p>A solo bookkeeper working with several clients. Often flexible and personal, with costs that suit smaller businesses. Quality and availability vary widely, and it's worth asking who covers the work if they are sick, on vacation or overloaded.</p>
      <h3>Online bookkeeping service</h3>
      <p>A software-led service with a large team, usually at a lower monthly price. These work well for simple businesses. The trade-offs can be a rotating contact, limited judgment on unusual transactions, and books kept in the provider's own system rather than in software you control.</p>
      <h3>Bookkeeping or accounting firm</h3>
      <p>A firm with a defined process, review of the work, and a dedicated contact. These typically cost more than a freelancer or online service, and in exchange bring more structure, accountability and judgment. Firms vary in size and focus, so the questions below still apply.</p>
      <h3>CPA firm</h3>
      <p>Many CPA firms offer bookkeeping alongside tax work. That can make year end smoother. Bookkeeping is sometimes a secondary service in a CPA firm, so it's worth asking how much attention monthly work gets during tax season.</p>

      <h2>Questions About the Work</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>What exactly happens every month?</strong> Ask for the steps. A complete answer includes recording all transactions, reconciling every bank, credit card and loan account, adjusting entries, a review, and delivered reports.</li>
        <li><strong>By what date will my books be closed each month?</strong> A firm with a real process will name a date. "As soon as we can" is not a date.</li>
        <li><strong>What reports will I receive?</strong> At a minimum, a Profit and Loss, Balance Sheet and Cash Flow Statement. Ask whether a written summary or a review meeting comes with them.</li>
        <li><strong>Who reviews the work?</strong> Ask whether someone other than the person who did the work checks it before it reaches you.</li>
        <li><strong>How do you handle transactions you can't identify?</strong> The right answer is that they ask you. The wrong answer is that they make their best guess or leave it in "Uncategorized."</li>
        <li><strong>What isn't included?</strong> Payroll, sales tax filings, bill pay, invoicing, 1099s and tax returns are often separate. Know where the line is.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Questions About the People</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Who will actually do my books?</strong> Ask whether you'll work with the person you're talking to, or be handed to someone else.</li>
        <li><strong>Will I have one consistent contact?</strong> A rotating contact means explaining your business again and again.</li>
        <li><strong>How quickly do you respond?</strong> Ask for a specific expectation, such as one business day.</li>
        <li><strong>What experience do you have with businesses like mine?</strong> Industry matters. Job costing for a contractor, inventory for an e-commerce seller, and tips for a restaurant all need specific knowledge.</li>
        <li><strong>What training or certifications do you hold?</strong> Software certifications, such as QuickBooks ProAdvisor, and professional credentials show investment in the work. They are a good sign, though not a substitute for a clear process.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Questions About Your Books</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Whose software will my books be kept in?</strong> Books kept in standard software like QuickBooks Online or Xero, under your own subscription, are easier to take with you.</li>
        <li><strong>Who owns the accounting file?</strong> You should. Ask directly, and get the answer in writing.</li>
        <li><strong>What happens if I leave, or if you close?</strong> You should be able to keep your complete file and history with no delay. Owners of businesses that used services which suddenly shut down learned this the hard way.</li>
        <li><strong>How is access to my bank and systems handled?</strong> Access should be granted through each system's own accountant or user invitation, never by sharing your passwords.</li>
        <li><strong>How are my documents stored and shared?</strong> Look for a secure portal rather than email attachments.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Questions About Pricing</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Is pricing a fixed monthly fee or hourly?</strong> A fixed fee makes costs predictable. Hourly billing can suit very small or irregular needs, but it makes it harder to ask questions freely.</li>
        <li><strong>What would cause my fee to change?</strong> Common factors are transaction volume, the number of accounts, payroll and added services. Know them in advance.</li>
        <li><strong>Is there a setup or onboarding fee?</strong> Ask what it covers.</li>
        <li><strong>If my books are behind, how is catch-up work priced?</strong> Get a clear method or a fixed quote before work begins.</li>
        <li><strong>What is the contract term and notice period?</strong> Know how long you are committed and how to end the arrangement.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Warning Signs</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>They can't describe their monthly process step by step.</li>
        <li>They won't commit to a date for your monthly reports.</li>
        <li>They quote a price before asking about your accounts, transaction volume or how current your books are.</li>
        <li>They ask for your bank or software passwords instead of setting up their own access.</li>
        <li>Your books would live only in their proprietary system.</li>
        <li>They can't tell you who would do the work or who reviews it.</li>
        <li>They promise to make your tax bill smaller. Bookkeeping should make your numbers accurate; tax strategy is a separate conversation with a tax professional.</li>
        <li>References or examples of their work aren't available.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Comparing Your Options</h2>
      <p>Price is the easiest thing to compare and the least useful on its own. A lower monthly fee that leaves the accounts unreconciled often costs more by year end, in tax preparation fees, missed deductions or a cleanup. When comparing providers, look at what is included each month, who reviews it, the date you will receive your reports, and what happens to your books if the relationship ends. Then compare price.</p>
      <p>It's also fair to judge the first conversation itself. A good bookkeeper will ask more about your business than they talk about themselves, explain things in plain language, and be clear about what they don't do.</p>

      <h2>Question List</h2>
      <p>A short list to bring to every conversation.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>What happens every month, step by step?</li>
        <li>By what date will my books be closed each month?</li>
        <li>Do you reconcile every bank, credit card and loan account monthly?</li>
        <li>What reports will I receive, and will someone walk me through them?</li>
        <li>Who reviews the work before I see it?</li>
        <li>Who will be my contact, and how quickly do you respond?</li>
        <li>What experience do you have in my industry?</li>
        <li>Whose software are my books kept in, and who owns the file?</li>
        <li>What happens to my books if I leave or you close?</li>
        <li>What is included, and what costs extra?</li>
        <li>What would cause my fee to change?</li>
        <li>What is the contract term and notice period?</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="muted small-note">This guide is general information to help owners compare bookkeeping providers. The right fit depends on your business's size, industry and needs.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
